WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.432
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
In a previous episode,

00:00:02.432 --> 00:00:05.970
we went over how logs are
aggregated across all of GCP

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.010
and how to navigate them.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.740
In this episode, we'll
talk about error reporting,

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.710
a Google Cloud tool available
to help you better capture

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:17.075
errors, categorize them,
and manage their lifecycle.

00:00:17.075 --> 00:00:20.398
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:28.270
Application level errors happen,
sometimes even in production.

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.190
And the last thing
you want is crawling

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:34.190
through your logs to find
the relevant information.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:39.170
Instead, cloud log's reporting
aggregates and displays errors

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.390
produced in your
running Cloud Services.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:46.190
It automatically groups together
error and critical level errors

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.530
from your application
into groups

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:52.070
and can notify you if a
new error group appears.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.670
This works across many languages
and is supported out-of-the-box

00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:00.080
by Cloud Functions, App Engine,
Cloud Run, Compute Engine,

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:01.880
and GKE.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.410
Any of your
application errors can

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.150
be surfaced using
error reporting,

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.610
provided that they
use basic formatting

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:11.560
or that they simply call
the error reporting API.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.660
Errors are grouped
and de-duplicated

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.240
by analyzing their stack
traces, so you only

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.940
see one entry per error type.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.040
Each error entry
provides a summary

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.680
that helps you determine
when the application started

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:26.620
to produce the error,
how often it occurred,

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.960
and how recently it occurred.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.970
One thing I really
like is the ability

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:34.480
to set a resolution
status for each error.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.130
It defaults to open and can be
set to acknowledged, resolved,

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:41.640
or simply muted.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.540
Errors can also be linked to an
issue in a bug tracking system.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.790
This is really powerful,
and thankfully you

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.310
do not need to stare at
the console in search

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.900
of potential errors.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.900
Instead, you can be
notified in real time

00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:00.240
via email, or my favorite, via
the Google Cloud mobile app.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.790
But maybe the most
important thing

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.220
about cloud error
reporting is that it

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:08.039
requires zero setup for
all of Google Cloud's

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:09.720
serverless products.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.940
And it's really just
a few simple steps

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.830
for other products.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.040
If you are a developer and
if restarting or redeploying

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.950
your application just
incorporate some new logging

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.710
statements sounds
painful, then be

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.050
sure to keep your eyes
open for an upcoming

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:29.160
episode for a truly unique
solution to this problem using

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.770
debug log points.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.840
In a future episode,
we'll also talk

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.390
about tools available to
help you better understand

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.480
the problem that led
to any of those errors

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.420
and, most importantly,
how to fix them.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.940
If you liked this video, please
like, subscribe, comment,

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:50.840
share, and look forward to more
Google Cloud Essentials videos.

